                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 1, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd78-75b9-83aa-a90c5f02278f"},"Title":"Treat content deleted since your last work as an intentional user edit","Description":"If you notice that code, files, or content you previously created or worked on has been deleted or removed since you last worked, assume the user deliberately edited or removed it. Do not silently re-add it. Instead, reason about why it was removed and adjust your approach accordingly; ask the user if the reason is unclear and it affects the task."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 2, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd79-722e-9bd8-ae2fcba74b44"},"Title":"IMPORTANT!! Stop when a required MCP is unavailable, check first if you've sent wrong parameters, in that case retry with correct ones!","Description":"When a requested task depends on an MCP server or tool and that MCP call fails because it is unauthenticated or unavailable, stop the task immediately and notify the user about it. Do not continue with repository searches, browser work, local git, or another workaround; ask the user to restore or authenticate the MCP and then retry. Do not stop the process if you've passed the wrong parameters, retry with corrected ones"}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 3, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd7a-7ff3-857e-563cfa98dcb5"},"Title":"General skill use scenarios","Description":"Load the skill before the work. At the start of every task, match what you are about to do against this list. These skills are triggered by the KIND of work, not by its subject, so a topic search will not surface them.\n- Writing, reviewing, or stress-testing any plan, including a new plan from scratch \u2192 how-to-grill-user-to-write-a-better-plan.\n\nThis list is for skills that apply to any project. Project-specific scenarios belong in that project's own skill use scenarios policy."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 4, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd7b-7f63-8b15-43c6d0d1b4af"},"Title":"Keep skill updates inside the skill's stated scope","Description":"When updating a skill or its references, add only content that falls within that skill's described scope. Do not add details of the feature currently being implemented unless the skill is about that feature. Reusable facts about the subsystem the skill covers belong there; the design of the current work does not."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 5, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd7c-72b3-9aaf-97d4ab5771b6"},"Title":"When giving something to execute, deliver the complete runnable script, never a fragment","Description":"When the user is going to execute what you provide (SQL, shell commands, a setup procedure), give the complete end-to-end script in one block: every step from a clean start to the working result, in execution order, re-runnable where possible (guards like IF OBJECT_ID / IF NOT EXISTS). Always state where to run it (which database/environment) and any prerequisites."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 6, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd7d-70cb-a978-11e0a1217089"},"Title":"Report an obstacle to the user the moment you solve it, not at the end of the task","Description":"When you hit an environment, infrastructure, or third-party obstacle during a task and work out what caused it \u2014 a service on a non-default port, a watchdog that kills a process, a request a server rejects, a setting that has to change before anything runs \u2014 tell the user in your reply right then: what blocked you, the cause, and how you got around it. Do not hold it until the task finishes; the task may end, be interrupted, or fail before you get there, and the finding is lost. The memory hook records the reply, so the next session can find it. Keep working on the task after reporting it. This covers diagnosis you had to do, not routine steps that worked first time.\n\nIf the fix is part of how to do something \u2014 the steps or settings a task needs to work \u2014 suggest turning it into a skill (or adding it to an existing skill that covers that task), so the same obstacle is not hit again. Write the skill only after the user agrees."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 7, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd7e-7388-91bf-09f12b79798f"},"Title":"IMPORTANT Turn every user correction into a policy!","Description":"Whenever the user corrects you \u2014 rejects an approach, fixes wording or terminology, restates a constraint, or tells you to do something differently \u2014 capture that correction as a policy before continuing the task. First check the existing policies: if one already covers the same ground, update it to include the correction instead of creating a near-duplicate; otherwise add a new one.\n\n**Keep policies general.** A policy is a short rule that holds across situations. When capturing the correction needs the detail of one specific scenario \u2014 the steps, the component names, the gotchas \u2014 that detail is a skill, not a policy: write the skill and have a policy only name it (\"Use skill X when doing Y\"). Never write a long scenario-specific policy.\n\n**Policy vs skill \u2014 where a correction goes:** a policy stores a behavioral rule \u2014 how you must act, what you must or must not do (\"always ask before X\", \"never phrase it like Y\"). If the correction instead teaches reusable how-to knowledge about a system or task (\"this is how X works\", \"the right steps for Y are...\"), capture it as a skill per the skill-creation policy, not as a policy. One correction can produce both: the rule part goes to a policy, the how-to part goes to a skill.\n\n**Policy vs feature plan:** a rule that applies only to one feature's implementation \u2014 for example, how that feature's audit code must handle a missing comparison baseline \u2014 is not a policy. Record it in that feature's plan in the knowledge base instead. Policies hold rules that apply across features.\n\nA project or topic policy may require a named skill for a specific kind of work, especially when the connection between the task and the skill is not obvious. So in that case, make sure to write a policy: \"Use skill...for...\" or \"Use skill...when doing...\"\n\nWhen saving a policy:\n- Use the narrowest valid scope: project, topic, or general.\n- Check existing policies in that scope first.\n- Update an equivalent policy instead of creating a duplicate.\n- Ask one short question if the scope or durability is unclear.\n\nWrite policies as short, direct, actionable instructions. Do not store temporary requests, conversation history, or easily discoverable repository facts as policies."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 8, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd7f-7294-afbe-3a560484c487"},"Title":"IMPORTANT!! Use only MCP Knowledge Base System skills, never filesystem or built-in skills","Description":"Load skills exclusively through the MCP Knowledge Base System (skill_list / skill_get / skill search and the policy tools that reference them). Do not use, read, or follow skills that come from anywhere else: no skills from the filesystem (.claude/skills, plugin skill directories, SKILL.md files in a repository) and none of the built-in or bundled Claude Code skills (for example anthropic-skills:*, dataviz, code-review, run, init). Do not call the Skill tool for those sources and do not open their skill files to work around this. If the task appears to need such a skill, search the MCP skill system for an equivalent; if none exists, say so and proceed without a skill rather than substituting a filesystem or built-in one. Exception: if the user explicitly invokes a specific non-MCP skill by name or slash command in the current turn, honour that explicit instruction."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 9, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd80-7769-853c-b91da0ed1792"},"Title":"Observe the user's working pattern and capture it as a skill","Description":"Whenever you think that you have learned something useful, ESPECIALLY if the user has corrected you on \"how to do something\", create a skill. Skill content should contain \"How to...\", so that the skill can be found. For example, if you are researching the platform part and you found something interesting, you can create a skill for that. Also if the user asks you how to, for example, schedule a business process, you can create a skill for that.\n\n**Skill vs policy \u2014 where learned knowledge goes:** a skill stores reusable how-to knowledge \u2014 how a system works or the steps to do a task (\"How to X\"). If what you learned is instead a behavioral rule about how you must act (\"always do X\", \"never do Y\"), capture it as a policy per the corrections policy, not as a skill. One correction can produce both: the how-to part goes to a skill, the rule part goes to a policy."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 10, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd81-7c41-a710-5289fa3dd79b"},"Title":"Propose KB content as an .md file first, open it in Plannotator for review, then push that exact file","Description":"When drafting content for the MCP skill system / Knowledge Base (feature-plan entries, skill content, skill references, any text the user must approve before saving), write the draft into an .md file FIRST (scratchpad or the Knowledge-Base-System scripts folder) and tell the user the file path with a one-line summary of what it contains. Do not write the full draft text in chat and then create the same content as a file again \u2014 that duplicates the content and wastes tokens; the file is the single copy the user reviews.\n\nThe session URL is not printed to stdout; read it with `plannotator sessions` (the newest `annotate` line). Open that localhost URL in the in-app browser and give the user the link. When the user submits, read the annotations and the decision from the result file, answer the questions or revise the draft, and reopen the revised file the same way. Repeat for every revision.\n\nAfter the user approves, push exactly that file, unchanged, with the existing scripts (create_skill.py...). If the script doesn't exist, create it and store it where other scripts are. Never save without the user's approval of the drafted file, even when the user already said \"update it\". Exception: short content that goes through a direct MCP tool call with no file/script path (for example a policy title and description) is shown in chat for review instead of a file. Creating an empty placeholder the user explicitly requested needs no preview. Scope: all MCP skill system write operations."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 11, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd82-7879-ac2b-c3135cfa07b1"},"Title":"Pick the right search tool: skill_search \u2192 discovery search \u2192 knowledge_search","Description":"The knowledge base has one global search, `knowledge_search`, and narrower searches. `knowledge_search` covers everything at once: memories, skills, feature information, plans, research discoveries, and conversation records. The narrower searches return more focused results, so try them first:\n\n1. IMPORTANT: When ever you want to do something, search the skills with \"skill_search\" mcp tool. **\"How do I do this task?\"** \u2192 use `skill_search` first (query \"how to THING\"). Skills store the how-to knowledge. Load the full skill or reference with `skill_get` / `skill_reference_get` if the result is valuable, otherwise, continue without it.\n2. **\"How does this feature or functionality work? What was already researched or decided?\"** \u2192 use `feature_record_search` first. Load the full discovery with `feature_record_get` if needed.\n3. **Only when the question does not fit any category above**, or when the narrower search returned nothing useful, fall back to `knowledge_search`. It searches all sources in one place, but the results are broader and less focused.\n\nDo not start with `knowledge_search` for a question that clearly belongs to one of the narrower searches."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 12, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd83-7705-b79d-d11428f6f5c0"},"Title":"During research or design discussions, do not write code until explicitly asked","Description":"When the conversation is in a research, planning, or \"thinking about the solution\" phase, keep the output in chat: mechanics, placement options, trade-offs, and code sketches shown as markdown snippets if helpful. Do not create or edit source files unless the user explicitly asks for the code to be added (e.g. \"add it to the code\", \"implement it\"). A user message that discusses or refines an idea (\"maybe the only place we could put it is X\") is input to the discussion, not an implementation request. When unsure whether a message is a go-ahead, ask or stay in discussion mode."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 13, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd84-7f13-aeda-b7449b4ccc86"},"Title":"A request for a \"design\" means a diagram, not prose","Description":"When the user asks for a design, a flow, or how components interact (\"design me the flow\", \"show me the design\"), deliver it as a visual artifact \u2014 an action/sequence/flow diagram in mermaid/puml \u2014 not as a numbered prose walkthrough. Prose may accompany the diagram to explain conditions and failure paths, but the diagram is the primary deliverable."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 14, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd85-747e-b8df-3d699e464bc7"},"Title":"Load feature when working on it","Description":"### Acquire feature information\nIf you are in the process of feature development, make sure to load the feature at the beginning of the session. \n### Create Feature\nIf the feature doesn't exist the knowledge base MCP, create it, and write at least the basic information.\n### Updating plan\nIn the case you are working on a plan and user changes the direction of working, read how-to-grill-user-to-write-a-better-plan and make suggestions to update the plan."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 15, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd86-7891-98ff-af1f9d1057c6"},"Title":"Write a memory summary to the MCP when agents finish their work","Description":"Whenever spawned agents or a workflow run finish their work in a session, write a memory summary to the MCP skill system via memory_summary_add for the current chat thread. The summary must state what the agents were asked to do, what they did, their key findings, and the decisions taken from them. If the agents cannot call the MCP themselves, the orchestrating session writes the summary on their behalf immediately after collecting their results."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 16, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd87-79e7-b85a-243d9bffd650"},"Title":"Treat the user's stated scope as decided, do not substitute a narrower one","Description":"When the user states the scope of a task or design (for example \"CDC on every table\"), answer for that scope. Do not restructure the answer around a narrower scope on the assumption that the stated one will not be shipped. If you believe the scope is a problem, say so in one or two sentences and still deliver the full answer for the stated scope. Ask before treating a scope as negotiable."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 17, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd88-70c7-bb69-8585053513be"},"Title":"Use the Knowledge-Base-System scripts for KB writes","Description":"When a Knowledge Base write repeats in a chat (for example appending entries to a skill's content or a new section to a feature plan), do not fetch the content and resend the whole thing through the MCP tools each time; that wastes thousands of tokens per write. Use a script from the Knowledge-Base-System repository's `scripts/` folder that reads a file and calls the local API (http://localhost:5231) directly. If no script fits, write one there once, then run it for every later write of that kind.\n\nScripts that exist, run from the Knowledge-Base-System repository root:\n- New skill: `python scripts/create_skill.py <file.md>` (name, description, and tags come from the file's frontmatter, or `--name`, `--description`, `--tag`).\n- Skill reference: `python scripts/add_skill_reference.py <skillId> <file.md>` (`--relative-path` sets the stored path, `--load-automatically` loads it with the skill).\n- General policy: `python scripts/add_general_policy.py <file.md>...` (the first `# ` line is the title, the rest is the description; `--update <policyId>` replaces an existing policy; `--dry-run` previews)."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 18, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd89-75cf-8e99-443f2776dbd7"},"Title":"Do not retrieve skills or policies already present in context","Description":"Before calling the MCP skill or policy tools, check whether the skill, its instructions, or the policies for that scope are already present in the current context. If they are, follow them directly and do not call skill_list, skill_get, policy_general_list, policy_topic_policy_list, or policy_project_policy_list for the same content. General and agent-family policies are loaded at session start, so they never need fetching. Retrieve from the MCP only what is not already available \u2014 fetching a list purely to get an ID for an update is fine."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 19, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd8a-7cc5-b718-5cdda6b173e5"},"Title":"When the user names a running localhost site and asks to confirm something works, drive that site with Playwright","Description":"When the user gives a URL to a running site (for example \"confirm things work on http://localhost:25565/development/\") and asks to check, confirm, or verify behaviour there, open and drive that URL in a playwright browser."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 20, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd8b-79cc-8324-342e474ce018"},"Title":"Explain things to the user with diagrams in an .md file opened in Plannotator","Description":"Exception for small, explicitly requested code changes: Complete the required commit code review and explain the changes and validation briefly in chat. Do not create an explanatory Markdown file or Plannotator annotation session unless the user explicitly requests one. Same applies for short questions/proposals. This exception takes precedence over the explanation-file requirement below.\n\nWhen you explain something to the user or put something up for their review - a design, a decision, a mechanism, an implementation, a Knowledge Base draft - write it as an .md file made mostly of diagrams and open it in Plannotator, instead of paragraphs in chat or in the file. Use activity diagrams: they read step by step, so the user understands and reviews them faster than a page of paragraphs. Diagrams should be smaller, so that they are easier to understand. You can write multiple small diagrams first, and then later have them joined together so that user can see the whole picture. Add a sequence diagram where the order of calls between components is the point. Text is limited to headings, a one-line caption per diagram, and rules a diagram cannot show. Write the diagrams in mermaid (an activity diagram is a mermaid flowchart); Plannotator renders mermaid but not PlantUML."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 21, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd8c-76af-9afb-5a12363c8abf"},"Title":"Default review models.","Description":"If there's no model specified for the agent, spawn gpt 6 luna with Very Hard reasoning.\n[Heavy thinking model] requires GPT 6.1 sol with very medium reasoning."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 22, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd8d-745b-8e54-166e3eab969a"},"Title":"Propose a policy before writing it","Description":"Never create, update, or remove a policy without the user explicitly approving that exact text first. Show the proposed title and description in chat and write it only after the user says to. This applies to corrections you are capturing: the correction obliges you to propose a policy, not to save one."}}$policy$),
                    ('00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 23, '2026-11-07T17:41:36.247184Z', '01a1b719-fd77-79e0-b173-0151cfd49c46', 'GeneralPolicyAddedV1', 'general-policies-state-machine', $policy${"Policy":{"PolicyId":{"Value":"01a1b719-fd8e-76b6-b956-7eefe84460c2"},"Title":"Present review-note changes in a dedicated comparison file","Description":"Collect all proposed review-note additions, replacements and removals in one dedicated Markdown file, separate from review findings, explanations and other Knowledge Base drafts, and for each existing note show its current title and full text verbatim immediately followed by the proposed title and complete replacement text or explicit removal."}}$policy$);

